IMPORT prelude

-- Decorations and Uniforms Act 1922 (Singapore) -- the whole operative Act: wearing or using orders, decorations, medals, badges and
-- uniforms without authority (s 2(1)), assuming a military title of rank or post-nominal letters (s 2(1)), falsely claiming
-- entitlement and supplying to the unauthorised (s 2(2)), the foreign equivalents (s 3), the two exceptions (s 4), and the penalty.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/DUA1922.txt. The deposit says the revised edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; SSO marked it
-- "Current version as at 01 Oct 2026". The Legislative History (not part of the Act) lists no amending law after the Law Revision
-- (Penalties Amendment) Ordinance 1952 (Ordinance 37 of 1952, item 55 of its Schedule, commenced 30 April 1955) -- only revised editions.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0132 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper
-- question yet. The Act has four sections, so the row covers all of its operative text. Not encoded: s 1 (short title); who is
-- "authorised" (the Act never says -- it is a bare input here); what is "calculated to deceive" (an input, via the choice of item);
-- and any subsidiary legislation, Armed Forces Council instrument or case law (none was retrieved).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Decorations and Uniforms Act 1922 -- the things it protects`

-- s 2(1): "(a) any insignia of any order instituted by the President; (b) any decoration or medal, or the ribbon of any decoration or
-- medal, so instituted; (c) any naval, military or air force decoration, medal, bar, clasp or ribbon; (d) any badge, stripe or emblem
-- supplied or authorised by the Armed Forces Council or by the President; (e) any uniform or part of a uniform of the naval, military,
-- air or police forces; or (f) anything so nearly resembling [any of them] ... that it is calculated to deceive".
-- s 3(1): "any foreign insignia, decoration, medal, bar, clasp, ribbon, badge, stripe, emblem, or foreign uniform or part thereof".
--
-- The foreign items below are deliberately CIVIL ones (a foreign order, a foreign customs uniform). Paragraphs (c) and (e) of s 2(1)
-- are not limited on their face to Singapore's forces, so a foreign MILITARY medal or uniform may fall under s 2 as well as s 3
-- (an inference; the text does not say either way).
DECLARE Item IS ONE OF
    `the insignia of a Presidential order`
    `a Presidential decoration`
    `a Presidential medal`
    `the ribbon of a Presidential medal`
    `a military medal`
    `an Armed Forces Council badge or stripe`
    `an ordinary regimental badge`
    `a military uniform`
    `part of a police uniform`
    `a lookalike military uniform calculated to deceive`
    `a lookalike military medal calculated to deceive`
    `a costume that would deceive no one`
    `the insignia of a foreign order`
    `a foreign civil decoration`
    `a foreign civil medal`
    `a foreign civil ribbon`
    `a foreign customs uniform`
    `a lookalike of a foreign order calculated to deceive`
    `a club or school badge`

§§ `2(1), 3(1) -- what the wearing offence reaches`

-- A lookalike is caught only by s 2(1)(f), which resembles things "as abovementioned" -- the Singapore list. s 3 has NO lookalike
-- limb, so a deceptive copy of a foreign civil order is caught by neither section (an inference from the absence of words).
-- An ordinary regimental badge is treated as a badge within s 2(1)(d) (an inference) that s 4(1) then takes out.
GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act covers the item` IF
    CONSIDER `the item`
    WHEN `a costume that would deceive no one` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a lookalike of a foreign order calculated to deceive` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a club or school badge` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the item is a lookalike` IF
    CONSIDER `the item`
    WHEN `a lookalike military uniform calculated to deceive` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a lookalike military medal calculated to deceive` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a lookalike of a foreign order calculated to deceive` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the item is a genuine protected item` IF
    `the Act covers the item` `the item` AND (NOT `the item is a lookalike` `the item`)

§ `Decorations and Uniforms Act 1922 -- the exceptions`

§§ `4(1) -- regimental badges`

-- s 4(1): "Nothing in this Act shall be construed so as to prohibit the wearing, use or supply of ordinary regimental badges or any
-- brooch or ornament representing them."
GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the regimental exception reaches the item` IF
    CONSIDER `the item`
    WHEN `an ordinary regimental badge` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `4(2) -- theatrical performances`

-- s 4(2): "Nothing in this Act shall be construed so as to prohibit the wearing, use or supply of any insignia, decoration or uniform
-- for the purposes of any bona fide theatrical or similar performance."
--
-- Read literally, the exception names only insignia, decorations and uniforms. Everywhere else the Act lists "decoration, medal,
-- bar, clasp, ribbon, badge, stripe, emblem" separately, so a MEDAL, RIBBON or BADGE worn on stage is not within its words. A deceptive
-- lookalike is treated as what it resembles (an inference: a stage uniform is a "uniform" for this purpose).
-- s 4 speaks of "wearing, use or supply" only, so it does not reach a title of rank, post-nominal letters or a false representation.
GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the theatrical exception reaches the item` IF
    CONSIDER `the item`
    WHEN `the insignia of a Presidential order` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Presidential decoration` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a military uniform` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `part of a police uniform` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a lookalike military uniform calculated to deceive` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the insignia of a foreign order` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a foreign civil decoration` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a foreign customs uniform` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item, `for a bona fide theatrical or similar performance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an exception applies` IF
        `the regimental exception reaches the item` `the item`
    OR  (`for a bona fide theatrical or similar performance` AND `the theatrical exception reaches the item` `the item`)

§ `Decorations and Uniforms Act 1922 -- the offences`

§§ `2(1), 3(1) -- wearing or using`

-- s 2(1): "If any unauthorised person uses or wears" any item in (a) to (f) ... "that person shall be guilty of an offence".
-- s 3(1): "Any unauthorised person who uses or wears any foreign insignia ... shall be guilty of an offence".
-- No mental element is stated for wearing; "calculated to deceive" in (f) describes the lookalike, not the wearer.
-- "Unauthorised" is not defined anywhere in the Act.
GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item, `the wearer is authorised` IS A BOOLEAN, `for a bona fide theatrical or similar performance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `wearing or using the item is an offence` IF
        `the Act covers the item` `the item`
    AND (NOT `the wearer is authorised`)
    AND (NOT `an exception applies` `the item` `for a bona fide theatrical or similar performance`)

§§ `2(1) -- titles of rank and post-nominal letters`

-- s 2(1), closing words: "and if any unauthorised person assumes or uses any title of rank in the military, naval or air forces, or any
-- letters after his name indicating the possession of any order, decoration or medal, that person shall be guilty of an offence".
-- POLICE ranks are not named (though police UNIFORMS are, in (e)).
DECLARE Title IS ONE OF
    `a military rank such as Colonel`
    `a naval rank such as Commander`
    `a police rank such as Inspector`
    `post-nominal letters of a Presidential decoration`
    `post-nominal letters of a university degree`

GIVEN `the title` IS A Title
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the title is protected` IF
    CONSIDER `the title`
    WHEN `a police rank such as Inspector` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `post-nominal letters of a university degree` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- The stage flag is taken and deliberately ignored: s 4(2) covers "wearing, use or supply of any insignia, decoration or uniform",
-- not a title. Read literally, an actor billed as "Colonel" in a play is within the words of s 2(1).
GIVEN `the title` IS A Title, `the user is authorised` IS A BOOLEAN, `on stage` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `assuming the title is an offence` IF
    `the title is protected` `the title` AND (NOT `the user is authorised`)

§§ `2(2)(a), 3(2)(a) -- falsely claiming entitlement`

-- s 2(2)(a): "If any person ... falsely represents himself to be a person who is or has been entitled to use or wear any such
-- insignia ... or uniform or part of a uniform". s 3(2)(a): the same for "such foreign" items. "ANY person", not only an unauthorised
-- one; no wearing is required. Lookalikes are not things anyone is "entitled" to wear, so only genuine items are reached.
GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item, `represented himself as entitled` IS A BOOLEAN, `is or was in fact entitled` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the false representation is an offence` IF
        `the item is a genuine protected item` `the item`
    AND `represented himself as entitled`
    AND (NOT `is or was in fact entitled`)

§§ `2(2)(b), 3(2)(b) -- supplying to the unauthorised`

-- s 2(2)(b): "without lawful authority or excuse supplies or offers to supply any insignia, decoration, medal, bar, clasp, ribbon,
-- badge, stripe, emblem or uniform or part of a uniform to any person whom he knows or has good reason to believe not to be authorised
-- to use or wear it". The list does not repeat paragraph (f): supplying a LOOKALIKE is not within its words.
-- s 3(2)(b): "supplies or offers to supply any such foreign insignia, decoration, medal or foreign uniform or part thereof" -- a
-- SHORTER list than s 3(1): foreign bars, clasps, ribbons, badges, stripes and emblems may be worn only with authority, but supplying
-- them is not an offence.
GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supply offence reaches the item` IF
    CONSIDER `the item`
    WHEN `a foreign civil ribbon` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `the item is a genuine protected item` `the item`

GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item, `had lawful authority or excuse` IS A BOOLEAN, `knew or had good reason to believe the recipient unauthorised` IS A BOOLEAN, `for a bona fide theatrical or similar performance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `supplying the item is an offence` IF
        `the supply offence reaches the item` `the item`
    AND (NOT `had lawful authority or excuse`)
    AND `knew or had good reason to believe the recipient unauthorised`
    AND (NOT `an exception applies` `the item` `for a bona fide theatrical or similar performance`)

§ `Decorations and Uniforms Act 1922 -- the penalty`

-- s 2(1): "liable on conviction before a Magistrate's Court to a fine not exceeding $400 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding
-- 3 months". s 2(2): "the like penalties as if he had been convicted of an offence under subsection (1)". s 3(1): "in the like manner
-- and to the like penalties mentioned in section 2(1)"; s 3(2): those "mentioned in section 2(2)". Every offence carries the same
-- maximum. The text says "or", not "or to both": read literally, a court picks one (an inference from the words).
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `wearing a protected item`
    `assuming a title or letters`
    `falsely claiming entitlement`
    `supplying to the unauthorised`
    `wearing a foreign item`
    `supplying a foreign item`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine in dollars` MEANS 400

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS 3

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence, `fine in dollars` IS A NUMBER, `imprisonment in months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the sentence is within the limits` IF
        `fine in dollars` AT MOST `the maximum fine in dollars` `the offence`
    AND `imprisonment in months` AT MOST `the maximum imprisonment in months` `the offence`
    AND (`fine in dollars` EQUALS 0 OR `imprisonment in months` EQUALS 0)
